---
import figure1 from "../assets/images/transformer.png";
import figure2a from "../assets/images/attention.png";
import figure2b from "../assets/images/mha.png";
import TokenAttentionInteraction from "./viz/TokenAttentionInteraction.tsx";

// The hero used to be static — the equation was KaTeX rendered at build time,
// with no JavaScript needed. It now carries the site's first hydrated island:
// `TokenAttentionInteraction` replaces that equation with the same relation,
// made interactive, so a reader meets compare → weight → mix before chapter 1
// rather than reading a symbol for it. `client:load` rather than `client:visible`
// because this sits above the fold and is meant to be interactive immediately,
// unlike the eleven chapters below, which hydrate as the reader scrolls to them.
// The component fetches its own data from `runForward()`/`constants.ts`, the same
// way every chapter does — the page never pipes attention data down to it.
//
// The paper's own figures sit beside the equation as an orientation cue: the
// equation is the mechanism, the diagrams are where it lives. Figure 1 is the
// architecture; Figure 2's two panels are the operation the equation writes and
// the multi-head wrapper around it. They are reproduced with credit —
// arXiv:1706.03762 is under arXiv's perpetual non-exclusive licence, which is not
// a Creative Commons grant, so this is a fair-dealing reproduction for study and
// review rather than a licensed one. Chapter 10's architecture diagram remains
// original work and is not traced from these; see CLAUDE.md.
//
// Three of them rather than one because of geometry, not appetite. Figure 1 is
// 1520 × 2239, so filling the hero's figure column with it alone makes it 990px
// tall; the two narrow panels stacked beside it fill the same width at a height
// the composition chooses. Figure 1 is first in the DOM and placed into column 2
// by CSS, so a screen reader leads with the architecture rather than with a
// detail of it.
//
// A plain `<img>` on the imported asset rather than Astro's `<Image>`: the
// optimising component needs `sharp`, and adding a native binary two days from a
// deadline risks the CI install, which runs `--frozen-lockfile` on Linux under a
// pnpm config that only allows builds for esbuild. So the original PNGs ship
// as-is. Width and height come from the import metadata so the space is reserved
// before they load and nothing shifts.
---

<header class="hero">
  <div class="hero__body">
    {/* The title lives in the text column, so the hero is two columns and nothing
        else: words on the left, the paper's figures on the right. It spanned both
        columns while the figure was a 320px thumbnail and the headline had a full
        1392px to itself; now the figure is the same height as the prose beside it,
        and a headline crossing above them both left the two columns hanging off a
        band of their own. */}
    <h1 class="hero__title">Attention Is All You Need</h1>

    {/* Under the title rather than above it. As an eyebrow it owned a row of its
        own across the hero's full width, which cost 39px at the top of a layout
        budgeted to one screen; in the body column it costs nothing, because that
        column is shorter than the figure beside it. */}
    <p class="hero__eyebrow num">Vaswani et al., 2017</p>

    <p class="hero__standfirst">
      The paper that replaced recurrence with a single operation: score every pair
      of words, turn the scores into weights, and mix. Everything below is that
      operation, run on one six-word sentence with numbers small enough to check by
      hand.
    </p>

    <TokenAttentionInteraction client:load />
  </div>

  <figure class="hero__figure">
    <div class="hero__figure-grid">
      <img
        src={figure1.src}
        width={figure1.width}
        height={figure1.height}
        alt="Figure 1 of the paper: two stacked columns. On the left the encoder — input embedding, plus positional encoding, then multi-head attention and a feed-forward network, each followed by Add and Norm, repeated N times. On the right the decoder — the same shape but with masked multi-head attention first, then a second attention block taking its keys and values from the encoder, ending in a linear layer and a softmax to output probabilities."
        decoding="async"
        class="hero__figure-img"
        data-figure="transformer"
      />

      <img
        src={figure2a.src}
        width={figure2a.width}
        height={figure2a.height}
        alt="Figure 2, left panel: scaled dot-product attention as a flow chart. Q and K enter a matrix multiplication, whose result is scaled, optionally masked, and passed through a softmax; that output is multiplied with V to produce the result."
        decoding="async"
        class="hero__figure-img"
        data-figure="attention"
      />

      <img
        src={figure2b.src}
        width={figure2b.width}
        height={figure2b.height}
        alt="Figure 2, right panel: multi-head attention. V, K and Q each pass through their own linear projection, h sets of them in parallel; each set feeds a scaled dot-product attention block, and the h results are concatenated and put through one final linear layer."
        decoding="async"
        class="hero__figure-img"
        data-figure="mha"
      />
    </div>

    <figcaption class="hero__figure-caption">
      Figures&nbsp;1 and 2 from <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762">Vaswani et
      al., “Attention Is All You Need” (arXiv:1706.03762)</a>, reproduced for study
      and review: the architecture, and the attention operation inside it.
      Chapter&nbsp;10 redraws that architecture from the page's own constants.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</header>
